1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,332
Made $1 million in a day. Went bankrupt more than once.

2
00:00:05,333 --> 00:00:10,666
At 14, his job was writing stock prices on a big board.

3
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:15,999
He studied how they moved. At 15, he made his first trade.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,332
In 1907's panic, he bet on falling prices and made $1 million.

5
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:26,666
In 1929 he bet on the crash again. About $100 million, reportedly.

6
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:31,697
Why a legend? He taught himself to read markets from prices alone.

7
00:00:31,698 --> 00:00:36,376
In 1907, J.P. Morgan asked him to stop selling. He did.

8
00:00:36,377 --> 00:00:39,999
A century on, traders still study his story.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,463
His key idea: the pivotal point, a price that starts the move.

10
00:00:45,464 --> 00:00:50,928
He bought on the break, then added only while it kept rising.

11
00:00:50,929 --> 00:00:53,332
Then he sat tight.

12
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:57,999
So how did he lose it? By breaking his own rules.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,017
In 1908 he followed a friend's cotton tips instead of the market.

14
00:01:03,018 --> 00:01:07,684
Cotton kept falling. He kept buying. Most of his fortune went.

15
00:01:07,685 --> 00:01:11,999
He also lost every cent he put into outside businesses.

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,043
In 1940 he wrote down his rules. Most cost him dearly.

17
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:20,948
Don't jump early. Never add to a loser.

18
00:01:20,949 --> 00:01:25,233
Trust your rules, not tips. Take some profit out.

19
00:01:25,234 --> 00:01:27,999
Learning them here costs less.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,332
Legend card 01. Learn from his wins, and from his misses.

21
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:41,282
